WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.363
A line at a ticket counter has one rule

2
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.976
the person who arrived first

3
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.650
is served first.

4
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.140
Nobody likes it when that rule is broken.

5
00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.433
Computers have the same rule in many places.

6
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.187
A printer works through its jobs

7
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.708
in the order they were sent.

8
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.867
A web server answers requests

9
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.690
in the order they arrived.

10
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.067
What is the structure that keeps

11
00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.240
things in arrival order?

12
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.590
It's called a queue

13
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.180
and it is the mirror image of the stack.

14
00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.160
In this video we'll see the queue's promise

15
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.620
a clever way to store it in an array

16
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.610
and the linked alternative.

17
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.180
A queue is first in, first out.

18
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.020
You add items at one end, called the rear

19
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.240
and you remove them from the other end

20
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.350
called the front.

21
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.672
Enqueue adds an item at the rear.

22
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.216
Dequeue removes the item at

23
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.135
the front and returns it.

24
00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.815
First looks at the front item

25
00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.090
without removing it.

26
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.710
And as with a stack

27
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.170
we can ask for the size

28
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.060
and whether the queue is empty.

29
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.552
Dequeue and first on an empty

30
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.090
queue are errors.

31
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.920
Enqueue A, B, C, D, in that order.

32
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.060
Now dequeue twice.

33
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.630
A comes out first, then B.

34
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.470
The queue now holds C and D

35
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.670
with C at the front.

36
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.020
The order of arrival has been respected.

37
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.504
The obvious way to store a queue in an array

38
00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.370
is to keep the front at index zero.

39
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.577
But then every dequeue leaves

40
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:35.926
a hole at the start

41
00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:37.904
and we shift every other item

42
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.800
one place to the left.

43
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.360
That's n minus one moves for every dequeue.

44
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.576
With a long queue

45
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.535
almost all of the time goes into shuffling

46
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.710
items that did nothing.

47
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.070
The fix is to stop moving the items

48
00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.900
and move the front instead.

49
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.599
Keep a front index.

50
00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.464
Dequeue just clears the cell and moves the

51
00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.530
front index one step to the right.

52
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.000
No shifting at all.

53
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.377
But now the occupied part of the

54
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.720
array creeps to the right.

55
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.097
Eventually the rear runs off the end

56
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.317
even though there are empty

57
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.510
cells at the start.

58
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.980
So we bend the array into a circle.

59
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.760
When the rear reaches the last cell

60
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.080
the next position is cell zero.

61
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.130
The array wraps around.

62
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.150
In code, wrapping is the remainder operator.

63
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.490
The next free cell is front plus size

64
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.400
modulo the capacity.

65
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.120
Enqueue E and it lands in cell zero.

66
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.500
Enqueue F and it lands in cell one.

67
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.094
The array is full, but nothing was moved

68
00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.318
and every operation took a

69
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.950
constant number of steps.

70
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.500
What if the circle is truly full?

71
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.763
Then we do the same thing as the stack

72
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.814
allocate an array of twice the

73
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.250
size and copy the items over.

74
00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.250
One detail matters.

75
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.750
We copy them in queue order

76
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.430
starting from the front

77
00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.190
and put them at the start of the new array.

78
00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.070
That straightens the circle out

79
00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.560
and the front index goes back to zero.

80
00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.698
The other way to build a queue

81
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.090
is a chain of nodes.

82
00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.950
Keep a reference to both ends

83
00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.500
head is the front, and tail is the rear.

84
00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.960
Enqueue adds a node after the tail

85
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.884
and moves the tail.

86
00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.271
Dequeue removes the head node and

87
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.340
moves the head to the next one.

88
00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.420
Every operation touches one end only

89
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.610
so every operation takes constant time.

90
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.850
Why add at the tail and remove at the head

91
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.550
and not the other way round?

92
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.573
Because a chain can only step forward.

93
00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.026
Removing at the tail would mean

94
00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.934
walking all the way from the head

95
00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.840
to find the node before it.

96
00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.010
To sum up.

97
00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.790
A queue is first in, first out.

98
00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.910
Enqueue at the rear, dequeue from the front

99
00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.700
all in constant time.

100
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.601
In an array, the trick is to move the front

101
00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.620
index and wrap around

102
00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.860
instead of moving the items.

103
00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.040
In a chain

104
00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.650
the trick is to keep both a head and a tail.

105
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.440
Next, we write both in code.

106
00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:15.947
Now the queue, in Python.

107
00:04:16.527 --> 00:04:18.897
Here is the circular array queue.

108
00:04:18.897 --> 00:04:22.157
It keeps a list called data, a size

109
00:04:22.157 --> 00:04:23.797
and a front index

110
00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:26.327
the position of the first item.

111
00:04:26.677 --> 00:04:29.007
Enqueue computes the rear

112
00:04:29.007 --> 00:04:31.087
the front plus the size

113
00:04:31.087 --> 00:04:33.025
modulo the length of the list.

114
00:04:33.087 --> 00:04:34.681
It stores the item there

115
00:04:34.747 --> 00:04:36.677
and adds one to size.

116
00:04:37.027 --> 00:04:39.297
Dequeue reads the item at the front

117
00:04:39.297 --> 00:04:40.557
puts None there

118
00:04:40.557 --> 00:04:42.757
and moves the front one step forward

119
00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:44.603
again modulo the length.

120
00:04:44.677 --> 00:04:46.921
That modulo is what makes the index wrap

121
00:04:46.977 --> 00:04:49.107
from the last cell back to zero.

122
00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:53.317
Let's watch it run, with a capacity of four.

123
00:04:53.317 --> 00:04:57.997
After enqueuing A, B, C and D

124
00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:00.807
the front is zero and the size is four.

125
00:05:01.157 --> 00:05:02.427
Dequeue twice

126
00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:05.387
and the front is two with a size of two.

127
00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:07.077
The first two cells are empty.

128
00:05:07.427 --> 00:05:09.897
Now enqueue E and F.

129
00:05:09.897 --> 00:05:12.577
They land in cells zero and one

130
00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:15.680
the data list reads E, F, C, D

131
00:05:15.777 --> 00:05:17.513
with the front still at two

132
00:05:17.577 --> 00:05:19.176
and the size back to four.

133
00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:20.956
The queue has wrapped around

134
00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:22.307
the end of the list.

135
00:05:22.887 --> 00:05:27.017
One more enqueue, G, finds the list full

136
00:05:27.017 --> 00:05:29.017
so resize doubles it.

137
00:05:29.017 --> 00:05:31.697
Look at the loop: it walks from the front

138
00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:33.305
wrapping with the same modulo

139
00:05:33.357 --> 00:05:35.310
and copies each item to index

140
00:05:35.377 --> 00:05:37.087
k in the new list.

141
00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:39.707
Afterwards the front is zero again.

142
00:05:39.707 --> 00:05:44.527
The dequeue order is C, D, E, F, G

143
00:05:44.527 --> 00:05:47.363
arrival order, even though the items sat in

144
00:05:47.427 --> 00:05:48.997
a wrapped-around layout.

145
00:05:49.577 --> 00:05:51.867
The linked queue keeps a head

146
00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:54.327
a tail and a size.

147
00:05:54.327 --> 00:05:55.927
Enqueue makes a new node.

148
00:05:55.927 --> 00:05:57.687
If the queue was empty

149
00:05:57.687 --> 00:05:59.487
that node is also the head.

150
00:05:59.487 --> 00:06:02.627
Otherwise the old tail's next points to it.

151
00:06:02.627 --> 00:06:05.097
Either way it becomes the new tail.

152
00:06:05.447 --> 00:06:08.097
Dequeue moves the head forward.

153
00:06:08.097 --> 00:06:09.657
And notice the last lines

154
00:06:09.657 --> 00:06:11.617
when the queue becomes empty

155
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:14.249
we also set the tail to None.

156
00:06:14.337 --> 00:06:16.443
Otherwise the tail would still point at

157
00:06:16.497 --> 00:06:19.007
a node that is no longer in the queue.

158
00:06:19.587 --> 00:06:20.897
In practice

159
00:06:20.897 --> 00:06:23.757
use collections dot deque as a queue.

160
00:06:23.757 --> 00:06:25.717
Append enqueues at the right

161
00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:28.557
popleft dequeues from the left

162
00:06:28.557 --> 00:06:30.707
and both take constant time.

163
00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:33.605
Don't use list dot pop of zero.

164
00:06:33.687 --> 00:06:35.698
It is exactly the shifting we

165
00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:37.047
started this video with

166
00:06:37.047 --> 00:06:40.657
every call moves all the remaining items.

167
00:06:41.237 --> 00:06:43.801
Next video: what if you want to add and

168
00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:45.827
remove at both ends?

169
00:06:45.827 --> 00:06:47.557
That is a deque.

